  "query_preview": "..(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 2:20 PM EDT THU SEP 3 2026\n\nSaturday will see troughing associated with an upper low over the\nSt Lawrence Valley/Quebec, extending into the ne conus to the Mid\nAtlantic. By late Friday night into Saturday morning, the \naforementioned cold front will sag s through much or all of ern \nKentucky, providing another focus for showers and storms. There is\nstill significant model disagreement regarding the timing and \nextent of convective coverage associated with this frontal \npassage, resulting in NBM PoPs likely being a bit underdone at \nleast at times compared to true rain chances, although uncertainty\nremains too high to refine these PoPs at this time. Adding to \nthis uncertainty, some deterministic guidance depicts MCS activity\nor its remnants diving se out of the Midwest during the day  \nSaturday, following the periphery of the ridge. Although rain \nappears likely at some point on Saturday, the details continue to \nremain unclear at this time. The NBM continues to keep pops \ngenerally low, maximized mainly in the 20-50% range, to account \nfor this. Meanwhile, nly postfrontal winds will keep highs in the \nupper 80s on Saturday afternoon, with low 90s near the KY/TN line.\n\nRain chances look lower from Sunday onward as the upper level ridge \nbegins to expand ewd. With surface high pressure building ne of \nthe forecast area, nely winds will keep Sunday's temperatures \ncooler than at the start of the period (but still quite warm). \nDewpoints look to be modestly lower as well (in the 60s rather \nthan the 70s), and this drier pattern will continue through the \nend of the period. Even so, Labor Day looks to be quite warm, with\nabove average temperatures in the upper 80s expected across the \narea. Other than this, the weather will be quite pleasant and \nsunny, with the exception of an isolated diurnally driven \nthunderstorm in s or se.",
